Confirmed. The rule in the code ignores which way an edge points. src/compound.rs judge() marks a subgraph `external` when an edge has exactly one end inside it: `for &c in a[k..].iter().chain(&b[k..]) { external[c] = true; }`. That covers an edge in from outside the same as one going out. The module's own docs say so too (src/compound.rs:7-10): "an edge across its border to or from a member (`A --> member`, `member --> sub` from inside) makes it take the direction round it".

Rendering with the prebuilt binary, `flowchart TB` with `subgraph s` / `direction LR` / `A --> B --> C`:
- with no outside edge, A, B and C sit in a row (A 42,68; B 156,68; C 270,68);
- with `X --> A` added, they sit in a column (X 42,28; A 42,152; B 42,250; C 42,348);
- with `C --> X`, also a column;
- with `X --> s`, still a row.

The same thing happens with `examples/subgraph.mmd`'s `GW --> Auth` cut down to that one incoming edge: `platform` drops its LR and lays out top-down (Auth 42,250; User DB 54.7,348).

The README only uses outgoing wording ("links out", `A --> X`). Line 283's "none of the block's nodes links to a node outside it" also reads as outgoing. It also says edges to or from the block itself are fine. So a reader will expect `X --> A` to keep the block's direction, and it does not.

The proposed fix is right. Below is a slightly tighter version that matches the module docs' "across its border" wording. Line 283's opening clause has the same one-way wording, so the fix tightens it too; the rest of that bullet is unchanged.

